> API and interpretation

All finite subject vertices and ordered finite rectangle bounds are checked,
including empty subjects. Zero-width/height rectangles are allowed; resulting
degenerate walks are retained. Checked native widened Cohen-Sutherland
intersections are reused rather than duplicated. No arbitrary convex clipping
polygon, self-intersection classification, deduplication or multi-component
decomposition is promised.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Sutherland and Hodgman, Reentrant Polygon Clipping, Communications of the ACM17 (1974),32-42|https://doi.org/10.1145/360767.360802]

